<p>Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu says any U.S. agreement with Iran should require dismantling Iran’s nuclear infrastructure—not just a pause in uranium enrichment—as Washington prepares for a second round of U.S.-Iran talks in Geneva this week. Netanyahu says he delivered that message directly to President Donald Trump, arguing that anything short of dismantlement leaves Iran with capabilities it could quickly restart. Iran, meanwhile, is described as seeking an arrangement that also brings economic benefits, pointing to talks that would trade nuclear constraints for relief or other gains. Diplomatically, Israel is setting a high public bar just as negotiators return to the table—highlighting the gap between what Israel says is needed for long-term security and what may be achievable in the talks.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/xyWxuFoUr43Tlz4byrY49Q6-Z4mtgKxF1trTTufoUREHbfCiEk6NxtgKLuEAV_P6Y2uyffo-PATAOGLJ_6bpWEvAMhaHJKeLvXbSDFRMOE44_qYM0CJolgLeTbYLg0YE25eA8IDKHR_icgsF4jUsPq93F7nTbBkjpMq1bEaHxVYYe5KwZLDpOUA8icdQHoBjf-T8htUV6mmdGQ3N_uaQly_E5dqPSZUEERkR2tcrjOuojPSoy4TDZQd-a02qXPlc7Qc9167l82VkVQJYAQ' alt='FILE PHOTO: Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu speaks at the Conference of Presidents of Major American Jewish Organisations in Jerusalem, February 15, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Broader demands: Netanyahu said an acceptable deal should eliminate Iran’s enriched-uranium stockpile, end enrichment capability, and address ballistic missiles.</li><li>Where he spoke: Reuters said Netanyahu made the comments at the annual Conference of Presidents of Major American Jewish Organizations.</li><li>Talks timing: The next U.S.-Iran meeting is scheduled for Tuesday, as part of a second round of negotiations.</li><li>Gaza tunnels: Netanyahu said Israel has dismantled 150 km of Gaza tunnels out of an estimated 500 km total.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu: Alongside his call for dismantlemen